  "markdown_report": "The recommended experiment remains a **terminal-loss curriculum within one BERT4Rec per category**, with constant-weight controls required to distinguish scheduling from terminal emphasis. At 10% progress, prioritize this attributable objective change before architectural expansion.\n\n**Insights from the supplied evidence.** First, equal branch counts do not guarantee equal loss weights: original BERT4Rec pools target losses, giving multi-target examples more weight. Explicit per-example reduction defines a different objective. Second, users reject broad associations and miss precise recent purposes or combined preferences; terminal-prefix supervision may help, but cannot establish compatibility or satisfied needs. Third, Electronics’ sampled negative-beats-positive rate is 24.46%; this motivates testing discrimination, without establishing generalization failure. Fourth, unavailable parity prevents sensitivity interpretation; introspection failure does not prove incorrect serving.\n\n**Insights from related work.** First, [BERT4Rec](https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.06690) already includes terminal-only masked copies, so terminal supervision itself is not novel. Second, [Recency Sampling](https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.02643) motivates objective alignment, while [Sequential Masked Modeling](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.11150) explores sequentially oriented augmentation; neither validates this schedule. Third, [Correcting the LogQ Correction](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.09331) motivates separating sampling changes from weighting. Fourth, the [replicability study](https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.07483) makes training duration an essential control.\n\nThe framework has three directions: **supervision alignment** changes target placement and weighting; **candidate discrimination** changes competitors; **context representation** changes recent/repeated-context interactions. Serialization parity underlies all three.\n\nThree atomic candidates follow. Scores are originality/potential/user alignment/model alignment/difficulty; higher difficulty means harder. Each includes benefit (+) and limitation (−).\n\n| Idea | Assessments |\n|---|---|\n| Terminal-weight curriculum | O 3: +controlled adaptation; −existing terminal supervision. P 6: +reusable alignment study; −schedule benefit unverified. U 6: +current purpose and preference conjunctions; −no usefulness guarantees. M 6: +tests weak discrimination; −training margins are insufficient. D 4: +small objective change; −paired controls and parity add work. |\n| Moderate hard-negative sampling | O 3: +targeted competition; −standard mining. P 6: +affordable; −false negatives. U 6: +plausible-alternative discrimination; −scores do not establish usefulness. M 7: +targets poor margins; −changes diagnostic difficulty. D 4: +shared scorer; −corrections complicate implementation. |\n| Relative-distance attention bias | O 3: +family-compatible context bias; −established technique. P 6: +tests temporal structure; −extra tuning. U 7: +recent/repeated context; −cannot infer satisfied needs. M 4: +testable sensitivity; −currently unavailable. D 5: +one network; −predictor/probe adaptation. |\n\nSelect the curriculum conditionally: compare equal weighting, terminal-only, a prespecified terminal-heavy constant, and a constant matching the schedule’s cumulative weight. It explicitly targets broad matches outranking current purposes and isolated attributes replacing preference conjunctions, while testing Electronics’ weak discrimination. These mechanisms remain hypotheses. Pair randomness, updates, supervision counts and checkpoint opportunities; record GPU-time discrepancies. Select by fixed four-category macro validation HR@10, treating validation gains as exploratory. Repair checkpoint parity before interpreting sensitivity.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Train one BERT4Rec per category with an explicit gradual shift from equally weighted terminal-prefix and interior-mask losses toward terminal prediction. Isolate scheduling through constant-weight controls while preserving the corrected backbone, user/endpoint sampling, negative sampling, optimizer, and serving path.",
    "motivation": "Terminal-prefix supervision matches appended-mask inference without exposing future context. It may reduce broad associations outranking precise current purposes and isolated shared attributes displacing preference conjunctions, but neither behavior follows automatically from changing loss weights. Electronics’ observed 24.46% in-sample negative-beats-positive rate motivates a discrimination hypothesis. Original BERT4Rec already includes terminal-only examples; the contribution is a controlled weighting experiment, not introducing terminal supervision. No supplied study establishes a benefit of scheduling over constant terminal emphasis.",
    "implementation_notes": "Define E as the configured epoch count capped at 200. For zero-based epoch e, use alpha_e=0.5+0.3*min(e/max(1,E/2),1). Compute its exact discrete mean alpha_bar=sum(alpha_e)/E; do not substitute an approximate continuous average. Prespecify controls alpha=0.5, alpha=0.8, alpha=alpha_bar, and alpha=1.0. At this early research stage, compare equal weighting with alpha_bar first; prioritize the schedule-versus-alpha_bar comparison before remaining controls if the 43,200 GPU-second budget is restrictive. Do not claim a schedule advantage without that comparison. Each experimental run trains one independent model per category and exports exactly one selected checkpoint per category; no trial checkpoints or predictions are fused or reused.\n\nPreserve the corrected host’s sampler. Specify and record whether it samples users uniformly followed by endpoints uniformly, or endpoints globally uniformly; these are different distributions. For the intended user-balanced implementation, sample uniformly among users with at least two training interactions, then sample target index t uniformly from 1 through n_u-1 inclusive, providing at least one preceding interaction and including the final training interaction. Endpoint probability is 1/(U*(n_u-1)); this is not uniform over all prefixes. If the corrected baseline differs, retain its policy across every weighting arm and report that difference rather than silently changing it. Remove the target occurrence at t and all later events; earlier repeated occurrences remain valid context. Keep only the last maxlen-1 preceding items, append MASK, then left-pad. Never expose target identity, text, or unavailable target timestamp through terminal input.\n\nFor interior examples, preserve the corrected baseline’s source-window and truncation policy and record both. Select a fixed positive number of targets without replacement, m=min(n,cap,max(1,round(n*mask_rate))), using the supplied cap, mask rate, and rounding convention; replace every selected target with MASK. Eligible examples must contain at least one real item. If the supplied baseline uses another guaranteed-nonempty mask policy, retain and document it across arms instead. Do not silently replace its policy with independent Bernoulli masking or zero-loss examples. Record eligible-user counts, endpoint counts, branch sizes, mask counts, and supervised target counts. The absence of baseline numeric sampler settings in this report must be resolved from the supplied corrected configuration before execution, not guessed.\n\nFor each example, average the unchanged baseline target losses over its supervised positions; separately average examples within each branch. Optimize alpha_e*terminal_mean+(1-alpha_e)*interior_mean. This explicitly gives each branch its stated coefficient and intentionally differs from original BERT4Rec’s pooled-target reduction. Keep this reduction identical in all controls. Keep the existing negative proposal and exclusions unchanged initially; do not introduce hard mining, LogQ correction, or new full-history exclusions in the same comparison. High-scoring unobserved items are not verified negatives. Terminal-only control may compute the same interior forward pass with zero contribution to match branch target generation and compute; disclose that its effective supervised gradient targets differ. Compare nonzero-weight controls with identical branch sizes, target counts, optimizer updates, and measured GPU time. Equal cumulative branch coefficients do not imply equal optimization trajectories or cumulative gradient magnitudes.\n\nUse preprocessing seed 0 and training seed 42, exactly 200 batches per epoch, the same configured E across controls, all four supplied datasets and routes, DATA_DIR=os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], single-GPU execution, and trial-local artifacts. Pair initialization and sampled histories, endpoints, masks, and negatives with dedicated deterministic RNG streams so control-specific operations do not advance the sampler RNG differently. Preserve optimizer, learning-rate schedule, regularization, validation cadence, and checkpoint opportunities. Use equal tuning budgets; record completed updates, target counts and GPU seconds, and disclose any early termination rather than treating unequal runs as controlled comparisons. The host’s fixed macro validation HR@10 determines selection. Record the prespecified final-epoch checkpoint’s validation result alongside the validation-selected result when the existing host interface permits, without modifying the evaluator or exporting multiple category checkpoints. Repeated validation selection remains optimistic; no additional seed search or hidden-test inspection is permitted. The historical 0.5425 score is supplied baseline evidence, not a measured result of this proposal.\n\nKeep final nonpadding-position readout under left padding, never count-1. Save architecture configuration, vocabulary mapping and size, PAD=0, MASK=itemnum+1, maxlen, positional conventions, and one selected state_dict per category. Expose a build_model(config) factory and generate the embedded predictor from the same canonical architecture implementation. Reconstruct with strict=True and verify exact checkpoint state tensors. Avoid inspect.getsource as a checkpoint dependency. Where dynamic importing remains necessary, register sys.modules[spec.name]=module before exec_module; this can repair source discovery but does not establish numerical parity.\n\nCompare the actual trained module, factory reload, ModelView scoring, and generated working/predict.py under eval() and torch.inference_mode(), in float32. Cover short, maximum-length, overlength/truncated, mixed-length batched, and singleton inputs. Use identical candidate IDs, truncation, mask placement, position IDs, and padding masks; preserve room for the appended MASK. Require finite logits, atol=1e-5/rtol=1e-4 agreement, singleton/batch agreement, and the existing diagnostic’s deterministic ranking checks. Report coverage and failures explicitly; these checks establish parity only for covered inputs. Preserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader) returning metrics and metric_definitions, diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis), and ModelView interfaces, including log2feats(sequence), item_emb, and predict_score(features, items). The loader contract remains (None, sequence, positive, negative), each tensor B x L. Leave sensitivity unavailable until parity passes; do not reinterpret approximately 0.51 embedding scores as collapse or training margins as validation gains.\n\nMonitor shortcut risks: terminal-heavy training can exploit popular targets or short-prefix regularities, and uniform-user sampling weights long-history endpoints less than global-prefix sampling. Use available training-derived history-length and item-frequency strata for descriptive diagnostics without changing the fixed aggregate selection metric. Do not claim ownership, compatibility, complementarity, satisfied needs, or simulator traits are measured. No extra datasets, pretrained weights, unavailable metadata, independent score components, or hidden labels are required.",
    "pseudocode": "E = min(configured_epochs, 200)\nalpha_schedule = [0.5 + 0.3 * min(e / max(1, E / 2), 1.0) for e in range(E)]\nalpha_bar = sum(alpha_schedule) / E\nprespecify_controls(equal=0.5, terminal_heavy=0.8, matched_mean=alpha_bar, terminal_only=1.0)\n# Execute only prespecified arms fitting the shared budget; no fusion or checkpoint reuse.\nfor objective_arm in budgeted_controls_and_schedule:\n    for category in supplied_four_categories:\n        model = build_model(corrected_configuration, training_seed=42)\n        sampler_rng = initialize_dedicated_paired_rng(seed=42)\n        for epoch in range(E):\n            alpha = alpha_schedule[epoch] if objective_arm == 'schedule' else constant_weight(objective_arm)\n            for step in range(200):\n                terminal, interior = sample_paired_baseline_branches(sampler_rng)\n                # User-balanced terminal policy, if supplied by corrected baseline:\n                # choose eligible user uniformly, then t uniformly from 1..len(history)-1.\n                # Input = left_pad(history[:t][-(maxlen-1):] + [MASK]).\n                # Include final training endpoint; omit t and all later events.\n                assert interior_has_at_least_one_target_per_example()\n                assert all_supervised_positions_are_MASK()\n                Lt = mean_examples(mean_supervised_positions(baseline_loss(model, terminal)))\n                Li = mean_examples(mean_supervised_positions(baseline_loss(model, interior)))\n                optimize(alpha * Lt + (1-alpha) * Li)\n                record_updates_target_counts_and_gpu_time()\n            apply_identical_supplied_validation_and_checkpoint_policy()\n        record_final_epoch_validation_if_supported_by_existing_host()\n        save_exactly_one_selected_checkpoint_and_configuration(category)\n    write_predictor_from_canonical_factory_with_existing_four_category_routes()\n    verify_exact_state_reload_and_covered_appended_mask_scoring_parity()\n    run_compatible_diagnostics_with_definitions_and_availability_flags()\n    # Host independently evaluates fixed four-category macro validation HR@10.\n# Attribute scheduling only through comparison with the matched-mean constant.\n# Export one chosen trial's single category checkpoint per route; never fuse arms.",
    "originality": {
      "score": 3,
      "positive": "Explicit branch normalization and a cumulative-weight-matched control make this a precise, attributable experiment within corrected BERT4Rec.",
      "negative": "Original BERT4Rec already includes terminal supervision, and curriculum weighting is established; a schedule alone offers limited methodological novelty."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 6,
      "positive": "A controlled comparison can clarify whether next-item alignment benefits from terminal emphasis or its timing, guiding later masking studies.",
      "negative": "Benefits remain unverified, user-facing mechanisms are indirect, and repeated validation selection can exaggerate apparent progress."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "The training change is a deterministic scalar schedule over existing branch losses, with no additional model at inference.",
      "negative": "Reproducible paired sampling, sampler documentation, compute controls, canonical predictor generation, and serialization-parity repair add substantial work beyond the loss change."
    }
  },
